roperties. The assembled symmetric supercapacitors achieve a maximum energy density of 42.1 Wh kg−1 (12.1 Wh kg−1 based on cell stack mass) in TEABF4/AN electrolyte. This work presents an effective technique for the formation of porous structures from biomass precursors. The novel methodology can be applied to many other similar systems for energy storage and beyond.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFenton Reaction Doubled Biomass Carbon Activation Efficiency for High‐Performance Supercapacitors (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/379752197_Fenton_Reaction_Doubled_Biomass_Carbon_Activation_Efficiency_for_High-Performance_Supercapacitors)\nciteturn11search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.5 years ago; Crawled: last month; DOI:10.1002/adfm.202403448 ... The enabled supercapacitor demonstrates remarkable cycling stability, retaining up to 99.74% of its initial capacitance after undergoing 20 000 charge–discharge cycles.\n\n# Fenton Reaction Doubled Biomass Carbon Activation Efficiency for High‐Performance Supercapacitors\n\nAdvanced Functional Materials\n\n  * April 2024\n  * 34(39)\n\nDOI:10.1002/adfm.202403448\n\nAuthors:\n\nYanyu Li\n\n## Abstract and Figures\n\nThe huge consumption of alkali during biomass‐derived porous carbon production leads to pollution and high carbon‐emission. This study employs the concept of Fenton chemistry to achieve hierarchical porous biomass carbon materials with a remarkably high specific surface area of 3440 m² g⁻¹ with double activation efficiency compared to traditional activation process. The optimized carbon electrode demonstrates exceptional specific capacitance of 425.2 F g⁻¹at a current density of 0.1 A g⁻¹ and great rate performance (286.1 F g⁻¹ at 100 A g⁻¹) in 6 m KOH electrolyte. The enabled supercapacitor demonstrates remarkable cycling stability, retaining up to 99.74% of its initial capacitance after undergoing 20 000 charge–discharge cycles. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFenton Reaction Doubled Biomass Carbon Activation Efficiency for High‐Performance Supercapacitors (https://ouci.dntb.gov.ua/en/works/422Q5364/)\nciteturn11search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.8 years ago; Crawled: last month; Journal Article ... 10.1002/adfm.202403448 ... Indexed in Web of Science ... The enabled supercapacitor demonstrates remarkable cycling stability, retaining up to 99.74% of its initial capacitance after undergoing 20 000 charge–discharge cycles. ... The assembled symmetric supercapacitors achieve a maximum energy density of 42.1 Wh kg −1 (12.1 Wh kg −1 based on cell stack mass) in TEABF <jats:sub>4</jats:sub> /AN electrolyte.\n\n# Fenton Reaction Doubled Biomass Carbon Activation Efficiency for High‐Performance Supercapacitors\n\n## General information\n\nPublication type\n\nJournal Article\n\nDOI\n\n10.1002/adfm.202403448\n\nJournal\n\n2024, Advanced Functional Materials, № 39\n\nPublisher\n\nWiley\n\nAuthors\n\nYanyu Li, Lifeng Ni, Jiayan Luo, Lulu Zhu, Xiaoxiao Zhang, Hongjie Li, Imran Zada, Jin Yu, Shenmin Zhu, Keryn Lian, Yao Li, Di Zhang\n\n## Details\n\nNumber of citations\n\n118\n\nNumber of works in the list of references\n\n2\n\nIndexed in Scopus\n\nYes\n\nIndexed in Web of Science\n\nYes\n\n## Abstract\n\nAbstract The huge consumption of alkali during biomass‐derived porous carbon production leads to pollution and high carbon‐emission. This study employs the concept of Fenton chemistry to achieve hierarchical porous biomass carbon materials with a remarkably high specific surface area of 3440 m 2 g −1 with double activation efficiency compared to traditional activation process. The optimized carbon electrode demonstrates exceptional specific capacitance of 425.2 F g −1 at a current density of 0.1 A g −1 and great rate performance (286.1 F g −1 at 100 A g −1 ) in 6 <jats:sc>m</jats:sc> KOH electrolyte. The enabled supercapacitor demonstrates remarkable cycling stability, retaining up to 99.74% of its initial capacitance after undergoing 20 000 charge–discharge cycles. In addition, the electrolyte ion distribution in different pore structures is simulated using Molecular Dynamics, which confirms that the structure is conducive to the rapid diffusion of ions, thus matching the excellent electrochemical properties. The assembled symmetric supercapacitors achieve a maximum energy density of 42.1 Wh kg −1 (12.1 Wh kg −1 based on cell stack mass) in TEABF <jats:sub>4</jats:sub> /AN electrolyte. This work presents an effective technique for the formation of porous structures from biomass precursors. The novel methodology can be applied to many other similar systems for energy storage and beyond.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFenton Reaction Doubled Biomass Carbon Activation Efficiency for High‐Performance Supercapacitors - Li - 2024 - Advanced Functional Materials - Wiley Online Library (https://advanced.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/adfm.202403448)\nciteturn11search9 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.5 years ago; Crawled: last month; 2024, 34, 2403448. https://doi.org/10.1002/adfm.202403448\n## Article Metrics\n\n### Full text views/downloads:\n\n6508\n\nTotal unique accesses to an article’s full text in HTML or PDF/ePDF format.More metric information\n\n### Citations (CrossRef):\n\n118\n\nWhen publishers deposit their content metadata to CrossRef, article citations are tracked and linked, allowing you to see how many times each article has been cited.\n\n### How to cite\n\nY. Li, L. Ni, J. Luo, L. Zhu, X. Zhang, H. Li, I. Zada, J. Yu, S. Zhu, K. Lian, Y. Li, D. Zhang, Fenton Reaction Doubled Biomass Carbon Activation Efficiency for High-Performance Supercapacitors. Adv. Funct. Mater. 2024, 34, 2403448. https://doi.org/10.1002/adfm.202403448--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFenton Reaction Doubled Biomass Carbon Activation Efficiency for High‐Performance Supercapacitors - Li - 2024 - Advanced Functional Materials - Wiley Online Library (https://advanced.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/epdf/10.1002/adfm.202403448)\nciteturn11search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.5 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Research Article ... Shanghai Key Laboratory of Mechanics in Energy Engineering, Shanghai Institute of Applied Mathematics and Mechanics, School of Mechanics and Engineering Science, Shanghai University, Shanghai, 200444 China\n\nVolume 34, Issue 39 2403448\n\nResearch Article\n\n# Fenton Reaction Doubled Biomass Carbon Activation Efficiency for High-Performance Supercapacitors\n\nYanyu Li, \n\nYanyu Li\n\nState Key Lab of Metal Matrix Composites, Shanghai Jiao Tong University, 800 Dongchuan Road, Shanghai, 200240 China\n\nSearch for more papers by this author\n\nLifeng Ni, \n\nLifeng Ni\n\nShanghai Key Laboratory of Mechanics in Energy Engineering, Shanghai Institute of Applied Mathematics and Mechanics, School of Mechanics and Engineering Science, Shanghai University, Shanghai, 200444 China\n\nSearch for more papers by this author\n\nJiayan Luo, \n\nJiayan Luo\n\nState Key Lab of Metal Matrix Composites, Shanghai Jiao Tong University, 800 Dongchuan Road, Shanghai, 200240 China\n\nSearch for more papers by this author\n\nLulu Zhu, \n\nLulu Zhu\n\nState Key Lab of Metal Matrix Composites, Shanghai Jiao Tong University, 800 Dongchuan Road, Shanghai, 200240 China\n\nSearch for more papers by this author\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInterfacial regulation of biomass-derived carbon towards high-performance supercapacitor (2024) | Bixia Wang | 30 Citations (https://scispace.com/papers/interfacial-regulation-of-biomass-derived-carbon-towards-34kym7yz96)\nciteturn11search11 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.5 years ago; Crawled: last month; Journal Article 10.1016/j.est.2024.111301 ... TL;DR: Researchers developed a method to enhance biomass-derived carbon's supercapacitor performance by introducing heteroatomic groups and increasing surface area through chemical activation treatments, achieving high specific capacitance and energy density with desirable cycling stability.\n\nJournal Article10.1016/j.est.2024.111301\n\n# Interfacial regulation of biomass-derived carbon towards high-performance supercapacitor\n\nBixia Wang,Wei Qin +1 more\n\n- 01 May 2024\n\n - Journal of energy storage \n\nPDF\n\n30\n\nPodcast\n\nSave\n\nCite\n\nTL;DR: Researchers developed a method to enhance biomass-derived carbon's supercapacitor performance by introducing heteroatomic groups and increasing surface area through chemical activation treatments, achieving high specific capacitance and energy density with desirable cycling stability.\n\nread more\n\nAbstract: Interfacial regulation for carbon-based materials plays critical role for supercapacitive performance improvement and the strategies about introducing heteroatomic groups and tuning specific surface area can be proposed to effectively address the low specific capacitance issues. Thus, the approaches about activation treatment with nitric acid (APC-H800) and copper chloride (APC-C800) for biomass-derived carbon materials have been raised and systematically investigated. Consequently, though the above chemical activation treatment, the oxygen and nitrogen functional groups can be introduced for APC-H800. Meanwhile, the specific surface area of APC-C800 has been significantly increased. Significantly, the upper specific capacitance of 384 F g−1 and 423 F g−1 for APC-H800 and APC-C800 electrodes can be achieved at a current density of 1.0 A g−1, what matters more is that two symmetric devices offer superior energy density and desirable cycling stability (no decrease that occurs after 10,000 cycles) are successfully assembled by employing APC-H800 and APC-C800 electrodes, respectively. More significantly, desirable energy density about 11.18 Wh kg−1 and 12.5 Wh kg−1 at 249.98 W kg−1 and 250 W kg−1 have been achieved for the symmetric supercapacitor devices of APC-H800//APC-H800 and APC-C800//APC-C800. These modification strategies shed insight into the sensible design of a further stage of advanced art carbon electrodes for powerful performance supercapacitors.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRSC\nSustainability\nTU  | TORIAL REVIEW View Articl (https://pubs.rsc.org/en/content/articlepdf/2025/su/d5su00439j)\nciteturn11search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; DOI: 10.1002/adfm.202403448.\nHazuddin and L. TaungMai, A review of bio-based\nactivated\ncarbon\nproperties\nproduced\nfrom\ndiﬀerent\nactivating chemicals during chemicals activation process\non biomass and its potential for Malaysia, Materials,\n2023, 16, 7365.\n220 Y. Li, L. Ni, J. Luo, L. Zhu, X. Zhang, H. Li, I. Zada, J. Yu,\nS. Zhu, K. Lian, Y. Li and D. Zhang, Fenton reaction\ndoubled biomass carbon activation eﬃciency for high-\nperformance supercapacitors, Adv. Funct. Mater., 2024,\nDOI: 10.1002/adfm.202403448.\n221 Z. Hrdliˇcka, J. Brejcha, J. Otruba, J. Skoˇcilas, J. ˇStancl,\nA. Kadeˇr´abkov´a, D. ˇCadek, J. Krmela and V. Krmelov´a,\nMicrowave activation of ground tyre rubber and its\napplication\nin\noﬀ-road\ntyre\ntread\nformulation,\nAdv.\nCompos. Hybrid Mater., 2025, DOI: 10.1007/s42114-024-\n01179-9.\n222 W. Ao, J. Fu, X. Mao, Q. Kang, C. Ran, Y. Liu, H. Zhang,\nZ. Gao, J. Li, G. Liu and J. Dai, Microwave assisted\npreparation of activated carbon from biomass: a review,\nRenewable Sustainable Energy Rev., 2018, 92, 958–979.\n223 F. K. Yuen and B. H. Hameed, Recent developments in the\npreparation and regeneration of activated carbons by\nmicrowaves, Adv. Colloid Interface Sci., 2009, 149, 19–27.\n224 S. K. N, D. Grekov, P. Pr´e and B. J. Alappat, Microwave mode\nof heating in the preparation of porous carbon materials\nfor adsorption and energy storage applications – an\noverview, Renewable Sustainable Energy Rev., 2020, 124,\n1